I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern shape, and the reusable analysis tooling in `learned/`. Then I’ll build a chemically plausible phase inventory, test candidates against peak positions and intensities, and only carry supported phases into a replayed GSAS-II refinement.

The chemistry points first to MgWO₄, with unreacted MgO/WO₃ and Mg–W–O polymorphs as the initial watch list. I’m now extracting the observed peak families and checking what GSAS-II automation and reference-structure sources are available locally before choosing records; no phase count will be assumed from the precursor list.

Peak-family screening now supports three phases, not one: wolframite-type MgWO₄, room-temperature monoclinic WO₃, and residual rocksalt MgO. Their diagnostic families occur at approximately 15.93/19.24/30.81°, 23.43/23.93/33.57°, and 37.30/43.21/62.54°, respectively. I’m using the room-temperature WO₃ record (P2₁/n) rather than high-temperature or high-pressure polymorphs, and will explicitly re-challenge the alternatives after a common profile is established.

The three-phase baseline is converging cleanly: after allowing the cells to adjust, the weighted profile residual dropped from about 24% to 14.4%, and all three phase scales remain positive. The provisional crystalline fractions are roughly 78% MgWO₄, 14% WO₃, and 8% MgO; I’m now refining only the common nonnegative U/W/X width terms and checking whether phase-specific broadening is genuinely needed.

The common profile is physically well behaved (U, W, and X are all positive and away from bounds), but the strongest low-angle MgWO₄ peaks show the same asymmetric residual shape. I’ve released only the axial-divergence term and am comparing that against a specimen-displacement model, lower/higher background order, and plausible fixed polarization values before adding any phase-specific broadening or texture.

The initial SH/L improvement was not replay-stable—the saved project continued moving when reopened—so I’m not accepting that intermediate minimum. The one-at-a-time size/strain trials likewise do not earn their extra parameters once replayed. I’m now forcing repeated save/reopen cycles until the common-profile solution itself is stationary; only then will I judge texture or minor-phase polymorphs.

The replay-stable baseline settles at Rwp ≈ 10.36% with a positive common profile (U≈55, W≈12.4, X≈8.14) and SH/L≈0.0318. Isotropic size/strain trials for all three phases are statistically unsupported (values smaller than their uncertainties; one strain trial becomes nonphysical), so those terms are being pruned. Texture axes are now being challenged from that stable baseline.

A single, symmetry-distinct MgWO₄ [110] March–Dollase term is strongly supported (1.0845±0.0033), improves several independent families, and is completely replay-stable; axial and other diagonal trials are much weaker or null. With that profile fixed as the common nuisance model, I’m now re-testing room-temperature, triclinic, orthorhombic, tetragonal, high-pressure, and equivalent-setting WO₃ records.

The unconstrained WO₃ source screen found the accurate room-temperature neutron model (COD 2311041) best, with triclinic WO₃ close behind and high-temperature/high-pressure forms much worse. However, every freely rebuilt trial pushed the common W term slightly negative, so those low Rwp values are not admissible. I’m re-running the comparison with W fixed at the physical boundary (0) and U/X/SH/L free; this keeps the source comparison fair without accepting a profile surrogate.

The constrained physical model remains best for room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ (COD 2311041): Rwp 7.58%, versus 7.64% for the low-temperature triclinic form and 11.40% for orthorhombic WO₃. Replacing placeholder zero ADPs with constrained common isotropic terms gives positive, well-determined values (W ≈0.0154 Å²; O ≈0.0207 Å²), but those terms correlate with the unknown effective polarization and shift the WO₃ fraction by about 1.4 wt%. I’m quantifying that model dependence before fixing the reported fraction.

With the final common ADP/profile model, triclinic WO₃ is only marginally lower in Rwp (7.332% vs 7.355%) and requires a much larger effective oxygen Uiso (~0.039 Å²); the room-temperature monoclinic neutron structure gives more restrained ADPs and matches the expected ambient phase field. I’m retaining monoclinic WO₃, while treating the monoclinic/triclinic distinction as a small residual uncertainty, and doing the last zero-vs-displacement/background-order checks on that exact model.

On the final common profile, MgWO₄ isotropic size is unsupported, but an isotropic microstrain term is reproducible and physically meaningful (970±102 µstrain). It lets the minor WO₃/MgO families share a narrower common profile while accounting for the broader MgWO₄ families, so I’m provisionally accepting it and checking whether either minor phase needs an additional complementary term.

The three-phase inventory is now stable: wolframite MgWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and rocksalt MgO. I’m doing the last residual-shape check, then I’ll replay the accepted model over the entire measured range and export every deliverable from that exact saved project.

The residuals still concentrate around the strongest modeled reflections, not at a new set of unindexed peak positions. A few peak-shape lobes are family-dependent, so I’m explicitly challenging the two strained phases with low-index uniaxial strain models before freezing the profile; this is the final nuisance-model comparison.

The anisotropy grid is still running cleanly; it compares seven crystallographic directions for each of MgWO₄ and WO₃ from the same converged isotropic baseline. I’ll retain a direction only if the added parameter is physical, significant, and replay-stable.

No variant has failed or hit an immediate numerical stop; output is buffered until the grid finishes. The broader search is deliberate here because an anisotropic term can otherwise mimic phase fraction or instrumental width.

The WO₃ [011] anisotropy is strongly supported (about 19σ for the axial component and a clear Rwp drop), while remaining well below its allowed bound. I’m now checking whether MgWO₄ anisotropy adds independently and whether any size term remains after those strain terms; unsupported size terms will be removed.

The follow-up is taking longer because each candidate is being saved, reopened, and replayed four times rather than judged from a single cycle. That directly tests the “reversible/replay-stable” requirement and catches parameters that only look favorable transiently.

The process remains active with no failed variant; GSAS-II is buffering the per-model summaries. Once it finishes, I’ll select the smallest adequate model, extend the limits to the exact measured endpoints, and do the final stationary replay.

One important issue surfaced: the retained WO₃ record uses an equivalent but highly skewed β≈136° cell, which makes two cell metrics strongly correlated in GSAS-II. I’m converting that exact neutron structure to a conventional equivalent cell before final refinement; this changes only the crystallographic basis, not the phase or atomic arrangement, and should remove the numerical correlation.

The basis transformation is correct, but the generated CIF labeled the transformed glide as standard `P2₁/c`; GSAS-II correctly rejected that mismatch. I’m relabeling the equivalent setting as `P2₁/n` (space-group No. 14), which matches the transformed symmetry operators, then rebuilding again.

The rebuilt conventional-cell project is refining successfully now. It uses the exact same physical WO₃ structure and mapped strain direction, but the full measured 9.903–100.095° range is active and the problematic skew-cell metric pair is gone.

The conventional setting removed the WO₃ cell correlation, but it also exposed that the added MgWO₄ anisotropy is coupled to the common X width term (76.7%) and prevents a valid convergence. Per the pruning rule, I’m rejecting that MgWO₄ anisotropy and retaining its simpler isotropic strain; the strongly supported WO₃ anisotropy remains.

The minimal accepted model is now fixed: isotropic strain for MgWO₄, uniaxial [110] strain for conventional-cell WO₃, no crystallite-size terms, and no MgO broadening. I’m writing `final.gpx` with three full-range save/reopen refinements—the last replay is expected to be the converged stationary state used for all exports.

The final replay is converged over all 8,301 observations: Rwp 6.739%, Rp 5.262%, Rexp 2.863%, GOF 2.358. The normalized crystalline fractions are 78.31% MgWO₄, 14.61% WO₃, and 7.08% MgO; I’m exporting CIFs, the exact profile arrays, and the machine-readable result from this unchanged project now.

The structural exports pass independent geometry checks: all three CIFs parse with the intended compositions and space groups, all occupancies are full, and cation–oxygen coordination distances are chemically sensible. I’m running one final consistency audit across `final.gpx`, `result.json`, `fit.csv`, and the exported CIF cells before handing it over.

